## Goal
Perform principal component analysis (PCA) on gene-level counts to visualize sample relationships and treatment effects. Generate a PCA score plot with samples colored by treatment status (treated vs untreated) to assess clustering and variance structure across the experiment.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| counts_gene | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv |
| design | 01_prepare_samplesheets | 01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv |

## Method
1. Load the counts matrix (salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv) and design table (rnaseq_bulk_design.csv) using pandas (gars-bio environment).
2. Filter genes: retain only genes with at least 1 count per million (CPM) in at least 2 samples, to remove lowly expressed genes.
3. Apply log2(counts + 1) transformation to the filtered counts.
4. Standardize features (genes) to zero mean and unit variance using scikit-learn's StandardScaler.
5. Perform PCA with 2 components using scikit-learn's PCA, retaining the top 2 principal components.
6. Generate a scatter plot (matplotlib) with PC1 vs PC2, colored by the `group` column from the design (treated vs untreated), with sample labels. Add explained variance to axis labels.
7. Write PCA component scores to a TSV table (samples x principal components).
8. All steps execute in a Python script saved under `scripts/` and submitted via the batch scheduler.

